                            *Expected results?>*

The submission platform must enforce strict separation of duties, ensuring that submitted research, zero-day proofs of concept, and PII are immutable, encrypted at rest, and accessible only to authorized triage engineers via audited, role-based access control (RBAC) logs. Human-in-the-loop audit logs must be immutable and tamper-evident to prevent unauthorized data exfiltration or internal re-submission by privileged employees.



                             *Actual Results?>*

Root-cause analysis indicates systemic failure in internal trust-boundary enforcement. Evidence suggests an internal threat vector where authorized personnel can bypass standard audit logs to access, copy, or monetize sensitive vulnerability research before public disclosure or patch release. This constitutes a total compromise of the confidentiality and integrity of the researcher/vendor submission pipeline, facilitating potential unauthorized exploitation of submitted vulnerabilities by external threat actors prior to vendor notification.



                          *Possible fix if any?>*

1. Implement immediate, immutable end-to-end audit logging for all access to unpatched vulnerability research. 2. Enforce Mandatory Access Control (MAC) requiring dual-authorization (m-of-n) for accessing unpatched PoC code. 3. Conduct a forensic human-centric audit of all internal account activity, cross-referencing researcher submission timestamps with external exploit deployment timelines. 4. Deploy automated DLP (Data Loss Prevention) monitoring on internal research repositories to flag unauthorized data exfiltration or copy-paste activity.


